 # The summarization model/provider is configured under auxiliary:
 auxiliary:
@@ -1056,6 +1060,16 @@ The value is the **first rung** of an escalating ladder, not a fixed interval: c
 
 `proactive_prune_tokens` enables a deterministic, no-LLM prune of old tool-result payloads that runs independently of `threshold`. On large-window models the `threshold` compaction (≈50% of the window) rarely fires, so bulky tool outputs (terminal dumps, file reads, web extracts) ride along in history and get re-sent on every subsequent turn. When re-sent history exceeds `proactive_prune_tokens` (default `0` = off; try `48000` to enable), the prune dedupes identical results and summarizes older oversized tool results, protecting the most recent `protect_last_n` messages and never calling the model. Tool-call arguments are execution records and are never rewritten by pruning; the summary model has a separate bounded serializer for copies included in its prompt. The pressure pass likewise demotes tool-result bodies only. During full semantic compaction the carried head/tail rows keep those tool-result demotions (so an oversized tail can still compress), while tool-call arguments stay byte-exact. The opt-in proactive prune still commits eligible tool-result-body demotions, so `proactive_prune_min_reclaim_tokens` (default `4096`) keeps those cache-breaking commits episodic; `proactive_prune_min_result_chars` (default `8000`, clamped to ≥ 200) sets the size below which a tool result is left untouched. This runs only under the built-in `compressor` engine; other context engines inherit a no-op.
 
+`tool_result_projection` (default `off`) is the wire-only counterpart of that prune, and the one that applies to your session's context window without rewriting it. Set it to `auto` to enable. Old, large tool results are replaced **in the request only** by a stub carrying the tool name, its arguments, the byte count, a content hash and the path to the archived output — the canonical transcript, session resume, the UI timeline and summarization keep every byte, so a `/compress` or `session_search` still sees the full result. The newest messages keep their full results: the protected verbatim tail is `tool_result_projection_tail_ratio` of the window (floored at 12K tokens), with internal message safety bounds (currently 8–60 messages) that are deliberately *not* derived from `protect_last_n` above — that knob governs what a compaction summary keeps, and its default would place most of a tool-heavy session inside the tail. Small results (`tool_result_projection_min_result_chars`, default `4000`), errors, multimodal results and any structured result declaring `projection_safe: false` are never projected, and a result already projected stays projected so the wire prefix is byte-stable between passes.
+
+Recovery is confirmed before anything is replaced. The full result is persisted to `$HERMES_HOME/cache/spillover/` and the resulting path is verified readable **by the agent** — the host path on a local backend, a sandbox-visible path on `docker`/`ssh`/`modal`. A row whose recovery cannot be confirmed (write failed, no live sandbox to translate or probe with, or an existing archived file that has since been pruned) keeps its bytes instead; replacing a still-usable preview with a dead pointer would make the model strictly worse off. The stub tells the model to read the archived file with `read_file`, and deliberately does not suggest re-running the tool — the projection cannot know whether a call is idempotent, and a `terraform apply` or `git push` must never be replayed because a result was archived.
+
+A pass runs only when it is worth its cache break: the newly stale payload (rows that became eligible since the last pass — a pile already projected is not charged again) must exceed `tool_result_projection_min_tokens` (`0` = derived from the route: 16K when it has no prompt caching, 32K when it does, capped at a quarter of the window), and on a cached route it must also cover the region the rewrite invalidates. So each break has to be paid for by stale bytes that arrived since the previous one, rather than by the session simply getting longer. The pass is two-phase: candidates and the economic decision come first, persistence and verification only for an approved pass, and the wire is rewritten last.
+
+:::note Opt-in by default
+Archiving old tool output is a **semantic** change to what the model sees, not just a transport optimization: the payload becomes metadata plus a pointer. It therefore ships `off`. The measured win is large and replicates across providers (wire tokens −77% to −90% in a 40-tool-call session, with the latency effect depending on how a route prices cache reads), but task-success parity on long coding trajectories has not been measured yet — enable it per install when you want the trade and can watch for regressions.
+:::
+
 :::tip Gateway hot-reload of compression and context length
 As of recent releases, editing `model.context_length` or any `compression.*` key in `config.yaml` on a running gateway takes effect on the next message — no gateway restart, no `/reset`, no session rotation required. The cached-agent signature includes these keys, so the gateway transparently rebuilds the agent when it sees a change. API keys and tool/skill config still require the usual reload paths.
 :::
